{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>מְמֻלָּח טָהוֹר קֹדֶשׁ</b> — that it be brined with Sodomite salt, as they said (Keritot 6a): 'Sodomite salt is a quarter.' And Onkelos translated it as 'מְעָרַב' (well-mixed); he intends to make מְמֻלָּח mean נִמּוֹחַ (dissolved), that all the spices should be ground very finely and mixed thoroughly until they are dissolved and no spice among them is recognizable — from the language of 'כִּי שָׁמַיִם כֶּעָשָׁן נִמְלָחוּ' (Isaiah 51:6), and likewise 'וּבְלוֹיֵ מְלָחִים' (Jeremiah 38:11), 'אֶרֶץ פְּרִי לִמְלֵחָה' (Psalms 107:34) — all of them carry the sense of destruction and wasting. And Rashi explained (Rashi on Exodus 30:35) that the term מְמֻלָּח means well-mixed through their being ground one with another, and he cited a parallel: 'מַלָּחַיִךְ וְחֹבְלָיִךְ' (Ezekiel 27:27) — so named because they turn the waters with oars when they steer the ship, like a person who beats eggs in a bowl to mix them well. In my opinion, the sages among sailors are called מַלָּחִים (sailors) because they know the taste of the sea, as if they are the ones who sense its saltiness and its sweetness — that is, those who know when it will be sweet and suitable for those who traverse the sea, or when it is bad and bitter for them. And the holders of the oars are not called מַלָּחִים, for it is written concerning Tyre (Ezekiel 27:8–9): 'The inhabitants of Sidon and Arvad were your rowers; the elders of Gebal and her wise men were within you, your repairers; all the ships of the sea and their מַלָּחֵיהֶם were within you' — for the elder sailors, those who know the sea, are מַלָּחִים. And it is written (ibid. v. 29): 'And all who handle the oar, all מַלָּחִים, and all sailors of the sea — they came down from their ships and stood on the land.' Behold, they are three: those who handle the oar, and the מַלָּחִים, and the sailors. And likewise, 'אֶרֶץ פְּרִי לִמְלֵחָה' — to a salty land, for a place of salt yields no growth, as it is written concerning Sodom (Deuteronomy 29:22): 'brimstone and salt, the burning of all her land; it shall not be sown and shall not sprout,' and it is written (Judges 9:45): 'and he sowed it with salt.' And it is possible that the lamed is superfluous in 'כִּי שָׁמַיִם כֶּעָשָׁן נִמְלָחוּ,' and it means 'they were wiped away,' like 'בַּלְאֻמִּים' (Psalms 44:15), 'כֻּלּוֹ שֶׁלַּאֲנַן וְשָׁלֵיו' (Job 21:23)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:23.857879157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}